course: religion-ethics
lesson: 87
title: Good secrets and bad secrets
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A good secret, or surprise, is happy, short and hides no harm, like Dad planning Mum a surprise party; a bad secret hides something that hurts someone or puts them in danger.
explain: If an adult ever says "this is our little secret, don't tell your parents", like a coach handing Ollie an old phone, that is a warning sign: tell your parents or another trusted adult straight away, and you will not be in trouble.
explain: A quick test: Will it make someone happy when it comes out? Is it short? Would your parents be fine with it? If yes, it's a surprise; if it hides harm, tell a trusted adult, even if you promised.
explain: The vicar says: "A good secret serves love and will gladly be revealed; a bad secret serves harm and fears the light", and Jesus spoke strongly about protecting children (Matthew 18:6).
explain: There is also privacy: a friend's adoption or a family's money worries are theirs to share, so "Ask her, it's not mine to say" keeps the secret without a lie.
fact: A surprise is fun, short, and everyone ends up happy; Jesus told people to give "in secret" (Matthew 6:3-4).
fact: Any adult who asks a child to keep secrets from their parents is showing a warning sign; tell a trusted adult and you will not be in trouble.
fact: Jesus spoke strongly about protecting children (Matthew 18:6); child safety programmes such as the NSPCC's teach children to tell a trusted adult.
fact: A secret that keeps a friend in danger, like stopping eating, must be told to a trusted adult; a friend may be cross at first and grateful later.
fact: Christians believe the body is a gift to care for (1 Corinthians 6:19).
fact: "If someone is caught in a sin, you who live by the Spirit should restore that person gently" (Galatians 6:1); a useful question is "Am I telling to help someone or to hurt someone?"
fact: "A trustworthy person keeps a secret" (Proverbs 11:13); refusing to answer is different from lying.
fact: Privacy (things that are just personal) is different from harmful secrets (things that hide hurt).
fact: Online, never send photos or your school to strangers; block, report and tell a trusted adult (online safety rules like CEOP's in the UK).
fact: "If your brother sins, go and point out their fault, just between the two of you" (Matthew 18:15): give a sibling a short chance to own up, and offer to go with them.
fact: "Everyone who does evil hates the light" (John 3:20).
fact: "Defend the weak and the fatherless... rescue the weak and the needy" (Psalm 82:3-4); a child's safety always comes before a secret.
fact: "A gossip separates close friends" (Proverbs 16:28); small embarrassing secrets are exactly what friendship protects.
fact: Slang check: "snitch" means tell on someone; "bruv" (London) means mate or brother; "kk" in a text means OK.
exercise: Dad tells Ruby about Mum's surprise party. Mum asks, "Why are you all whispering?" What should Ruby do? | Keep the surprise with a grin, dodging without lying if she can | Tell Mum everything right away, because all secrets are really just lies | Cry until Mum guesses what is going on | Tell Mum they're planning to move house
exercise: A coach gives Ollie a phone: "This is our little secret. Don't tell your parents." What should Ollie do? | Tell his parents or another trusted adult straight away | Keep the secret, because the coach is a grown-up and knows best | Keep the phone but hide it under his bed so nobody ever sees it | Ask the coach for a better phone, since it's only between them
exercise: Why is a grown-up asking a child to hide things from parents a warning sign? | Caring adults never ask children to hide things from their parents | Because coaches are not allowed to own phones | Because all presents must be shown to teachers | It isn't a warning sign if the present is nice
exercise: Isla says, "Promise you won't tell. I've stopped eating lunch so I get skinnier." What should Grace do? | Tell a trusted adult, and tell Isla it's because she cares | Keep the promise, because a promise is a promise, whatever is going on | Stop eating lunch too, to keep her company | Tell the whole class so they can help
exercise: An older boy says, "Bruv, don't snitch, it's only a vape." (A vape is an e-cigarette, harmful for children.) Rayan saw his friend Zak try it. What is best? | Talk to Zak first, and tell an adult if older kids are giving vapes out | Say nothing ever, because snitching is the worst thing that anybody can do | Try the vape too, so he is not left out | Post a video of Zak in the group chat
exercise: What question helps you decide whether telling is snitching or caring? | Am I telling to help someone or to hurt someone? | Will telling make me more popular with the others? | Is the person older or bigger than me, or more popular? | Will anybody ever find out that it was me who told?
exercise: Chloe told Mia she's adopted. Kids ask Mia, "Is it true?" What keeps the secret without a lie? | Ask her, it's not mine to say | No, Chloe isn't adopted at all | Yes, and here's everything she told me | I don't know who Chloe is
exercise: Mum says, "Don't tell Nan," about family money worries. Is that a bad secret? | No; it's private family business, not hiding harm | Yes; every family secret is a bad secret | Yes; Nan must be told everything at once, or it's a lie | No, because Nan is too old to understand
exercise: An online player writes, "ur my bestie, send me a pic of ur school and dont tell ur mum kk?" What should you do? | Send nothing, tell a parent, and report the player | Send just one picture, then stop talking to them for good | Send a picture of a different school instead, to trick them | Keep chatting with them but never tell anybody about it
exercise: Kate's little brother broke Dad's watch: "Please don't tell, I'll fix it." He can't. What is best? | Give him a short time to tell Dad and offer to go with him | Keep the secret for ever, because he asked so nicely and he's little | Tell Dad straight away in front of everyone | Try to fix the watch herself and hide it
exercise: Nan in Dublin says, "That's between us, pet. A surprise holiday for yer mam." What kind of secret is this? | A happy surprise: short, soon told and hiding no harm | A bad secret that must be told to Mum now, before the holiday | A dangerous secret for the police | A lie that Nan is telling to Liam
exercise: Jamie's friend says, "My mum's boyfriend shouts and throws stuff. Don't tell anyone." What should Jamie do? | Tell a trusted adult today, to keep the friend safe | Keep the promise, because family is private | Tell the boyfriend to stop it himself | Wait a few months to see if it gets better
exercise: At a sleepover Ava is about to tell everyone Sophie still has a night light. What should she do? | Keep it; small secrets are what friendship protects | Tell everyone, because it's only a little thing anyway | Tell just one person, because telling one person doesn't count | Ask the whole group to vote on whether she should tell them
exercise: What does John 3:20 say? | Everyone who does evil hates the light | Everyone who keeps secrets is evil | Light is only for good people | Everyone who tells secrets loves the light
exercise: Which of these is a surprise, not a bad secret? | A present for Dad's birthday next week | An adult asking you to hide a gift from your parents | A friend who is being hurt at home | A stranger online asking for your photo
exercise: Who is a trusted adult? | A parent, teacher or youth leader who keeps you safe | A stranger online who says they're a kid too and is very kind | An older teenager who says "keep it between us" and buys you sweets | Anyone at all, as long as they are grown up and say they're nice
test: Your mate says, "All secrets are lies, so you can't even keep a surprise party secret." What is the best answer? | A surprise is happy, short and hides no harm, so keeping it is fine | He's right, every secret must be told at once, even a nice surprise party | Only birthday secrets are allowed, nothing else | Secrets are fine as long as you never tell anyone
test: "If you tell your mum about the phone, you're off the team," says the coach. What is the right thing? | Tell a trusted adult anyway; a threat like that is a big warning sign | Keep quiet so you stay on the team | Give the phone back and say nothing | Ask your friends what they would do first
test: Isla shouts, "You told Miss? I hate you, you snake!" after Grace got help for her. Did Grace do wrong? | No; she kept her friend safe, and Isla may be grateful later | Yes; she broke a promise, so she's a snake | Yes; she should have stopped eating too | No, but she should say sorry to Isla and take back what she told
test: "Snitches get stitches," says an older kid about the vapes. What is really going on? | It's a threat meant to scare people off telling about harm; tell an adult | It's a fair rule that everyone should follow | It means snitching is good for your health | It's a joke, so nothing needs doing
test: Kids ask, "Is it true Chloe's adopted? Go on, tell us." Mia says, "No." What was better? | "Ask her, it's not mine to say" keeps the secret without a lie | Saying "yes" and explaining everything | Saying "no" was perfect and honest, because it kept the secret safe | Running away so nobody can ask her
test: A cartoon shows a friendly man in a game asking a girl to send her address "as a surprise, don't tell ur mum", and she does. What is wrong? | Never share where you live; tell a parent and report it | Nothing; surprises are always good | She should have sent a photo instead | She should have asked her friends at school first, before sending it
test: Ben's mum says, "Don't tell Nan about the money, alright?" Ben asks, "Is that a bad secret?" What is the best answer? | It's privacy, not harm; but if something ever frightens you, tell someone you trust | Yes, all family secrets are bad secrets | Yes, and he must tell the school straight away | No, and he should never talk to anyone about anything
test: Kate's brother says, "Just wait till Christmas, then I'll tell Dad." It's March. What's wrong? | A fair chance is short; months of hiding is not owning up | Nothing; Christmas is a lovely time to own up, so waiting is fine | He should wait till next Christmas instead | He should tell Nan instead of Dad
test: What would happen if children kept every secret adults asked them to keep? | Adults who harm children could hide it more easily | Children would be much safer than before, with fewer worries | Nothing; adults never ask children to keep bad secrets | Parents would always find out anyway, so it wouldn't matter
test: Jamie's friend begs, "Promise, swear on your life." Jamie promised. What wins? | The friend's safety wins over the promise; tell a trusted adult | The promise wins, so Jamie must say nothing, because he swore on his life | Jamie should tell the friend's mum's boyfriend | Jamie should ask other kids what to do
test: Liam's nan says, "That's between us, pet," about Mum's surprise holiday. Is it like the coach's secret? | No; it's a happy surprise that'll be told soon and hides no harm | Yes; all grown-up secrets are warning signs, even surprise holidays | Yes; Liam must tell Mum at once | No, because Nan is the one paying
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the distinction between a surprise and a harmful secret? | The former is brief, occasions happiness when disclosed and conceals no harm; the latter conceals harm | The former is invariably kept by adults; the latter invariably by children | The former must always be disclosed immediately; the latter may be kept indefinitely | There is no distinction, both being forms of deception
exam: Given that an adult requests a child to conceal a matter from the child's parents, identify the course of action consistent with established child-safety guidance. | Disclosure to the parents or another trusted adult without delay | Preservation of the confidence in deference to the adult's authority | Consultation with the requesting adult before any disclosure | Abstention from any action unless the matter appears dangerous
exam: Select the response which preserves another's confidence without recourse to falsehood. | A statement that the matter is not one's own to disclose | A denial of the matter contrary to one's own knowledge of the truth | A full disclosure of the matter to all enquirers | A fabricated alternative account of the matter
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning a promise of secrecy that conceals danger to a child is consistent with this lesson. | The safety of the child outweighs the promise, and a trusted adult ought to be informed | The promise is absolute and must be kept irrespective of the danger | The promise may be broken only with the consent of the person endangered | The promise lapses solely upon the expiry of one calendar month
